{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`caused_by` auto-derivation falls back to best-effort `git blame` over a bug's impacted paths\nwhen no `--caused-by` is supplied (`rebar.metrics.blame.derive_caused_by`, invoked from\n`_commands/transition_close.py::_resolve_caused_by_culprit` only when the bug carries no\n`caused_by` edge yet). The edge feeds `rebar metrics`' escape-rate lenses and the epic-close\n`caused_by` floor, and a WRONG edge is worse than an absent one: it silently charges an escaped\ndefect to a ticket whose reviewers could not plausibly have caught it.\n\nWhether the file-level auto-derivation is accurate for this codebase's dominant defect shapes is\nan OPEN QUESTION with no direct evidence either way — see the two correction comments below: the\noriginal \"usually wrong\" claim conflated hand-run line-level `git blame` with the file-level\nauto-derivation, and the first retraction's counter-evidence (\"blame ran on four closes and\nagreed\") was itself void because the `--caused-by` flag worked and blame never ran. The proven\npart is only that the dominant defect shapes here (unmet obligations, carrier commits, omissions)\nare structurally hard for blame. This ticket measures rather than asserts.\n\n## Approach\n\nMeasure the actual mechanism (`derive_caused_by`) against the actual store, read-only:\n\n1. **Enumerate the population.** Reduce every ticket in the tracker; select closed `bug` tickets\n   whose reduced state carries at least one active `caused_by` edge. Report the population size.\n2. **Re-run the real deriver on each.** For every sampled bug, call\n   `rebar.metrics.blame.derive_caused_by(bug_id, repo_root, tracker)` in this worktree (full git\n   history, current HEAD) and compare its output to the recorded edge target:\n   agreement / disagreement / abstention (`None`). Because the recorded edge carries NO\n   derived-vs-explicit marker (`_apply_caused_by` writes via `_write_link_event` with no\n   provenance field — verified at src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py), this classification\n   BOUNDS rather than identifies the blame-derived population, and the findings must say so:\n   agreement means \"consistent with blame-derivation\" (a supplied edge can coincide with\n   blame); disagreement/abstention means \"not reproducible by the deriver TODAY\", which is\n   evidence of explicit supply but not proof — the deriver abstains or diverges for reasons\n   unrelated to supply (the fixing commit no longer resolving, `file_impact` paths since\n   renamed/deleted, the pre-fix blame no longer yielding a strict >50% majority after\n   reformat/move carriers landed on top, or the culprit commit's trailer no longer resolving).\n   Each non-agreement case is therefore annotated with the deriver's observable failure reason\n   (no-fixing-commit / no-file-impact / no-majority / unresolvable-culprit / different-target),\n   and the classification is reported as a bound with those caveats, never as a\n   supplied-vs-derived truth.\n3. **Score accuracy against proven origins.** For the subsample of bugs whose ticket record\n   independently establishes the true origin (a root-cause comment or description naming the\n   introducing ticket — the `/rebar-debug` protocol writes these), compare the deriver's output\n   to that proven origin and classify each case: correct / wrong-target / abstained. Classify\n   each wrong/abstained case by defect shape (unmet obligation / carrier commit / omission /\n   other) so the rate connects to the shapes hypothesis.\n4. **Record the two decisions** the measurement informs, with reasoning, as a findings comment:\n   (a) should a blame-derived edge be distinguishable on read from a supplied one;\n   (b) should the fallback decline to attribute for shapes it cannot get right.\n   Any resulting schema change or review-time criterion is FILED as its own ticket linked\n   `discovered_from` this one — gate/criterion changes are operator-adjudicated, so those\n   tickets are proposals for the operator, not work performed here.\n\nDefault assumptions (stated so no executor question remains): the sample is the WHOLE\npopulation of closed bugs with `caused_by` edges (no sampling bias); a proven origin exists\nonly where the ticket record names one explicitly (no inferring ground truth from blame\nitself); history drift (a bug fixed before later rebases/moves) counts as a named limitation,\nnot an error, when the deriver abstains for lack of the fixing commit.\n\n## Scope\n\nNo repository file changes: measurement scripts run ad-hoc in the worktree interpreter against\nthe store and git history; results, methodology, and decisions are recorded on this ticket.\nFollow-up implementation work (schema marker, deriver behaviour change, review criterion) is\nfiled as separate tickets, not done here.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n\nThe deliverable is a measurement, so verification is reproducibility of the numbers: the\nfindings comment must quote the exact probe commands/snippets run (a Python snippet importing\n`rebar.metrics.blame.derive_caused_by` and `rebar.reducer.reduce_ticket`, executed with this\nworktree's `.venv` interpreter against the live tracker and git history) together with their\nraw counts, so any reader can re-run the same snippet and reproduce the\nagreement/disagreement/abstention table. The proving command for each numeric claim is that\nquoted snippet; the proving evidence for each decision is its recorded reasoning referencing\nthose numbers.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The rate is measured, not estimated: the population of closed bugs with\n      `caused_by` edges is enumerated (size reported), the real `derive_caused_by` is re-run on\n      each, and the agreement/disagreement/abstention counts are reported in a findings comment\n      on this ticket, with the method stated — checked by reading the comment.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] For every bug in the subsample with an independently proven origin, the\n      deriver's output is scored correct / wrong-target / abstained and classified by defect\n      shape; the proportion of blame-derivable edges naming a defensibly wrong origin is\n      reported with the subsample size — checked by reading the comment.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A decision is recorded on whether a blame-derived edge should be\n      distinguishable from an explicitly supplied one on read, with reasoning — checked by\n      reading the comment.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A decision is recorded on whether the fallback should decline to attribute\n      for the shapes it cannot get right, rather than guessing, with reasoning — checked by\n      reading the comment.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Any proposed review-time criterion or schema change is filed as its own\n      ticket linked `discovered_from` this one rather than implemented here — checked by the\n      links on this ticket."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787389022644287001,"uuid":"5c2e4aa0-928a-421f-8584-8a9644f52f4d"}